      "id": 1,
      "text": "Brownsville was named after Major Jacob Brown, who was killed defending Fort Brown during the Mexican‑American War.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20,
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://brownsvillehistory.org/discover-brownsville-history-brownsville-historical-association.html",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The first two battles of the Mexican‑American War, Palo Alto and Resaca de la Palma, were fought within present‑day Brownsville.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        23,
        24,
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://brownsvillehistory.org/discover-brownsville-history-brownsville-historical-association.html",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Brownsville’s historic downtown still contains structures dating back to 1848, the year the city was founded after Charles Stillman purchased land near Fort Brown.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28,
        29
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://brownsvillehistory.org/discover-brownsville-history-brownsville-historical-association.html",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Stillman House Museum in Brownsville showcases artifacts from the Gold Rush era and the early settlement period.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://brownsvillehistory.org/discover-brownsville-history-brownsville-historical-association.html",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Today, only skeletal structures and sealed mine shafts remain of the original mining community, drawing curious visitors to the ghost town site.",
